## Correction pass, 2026-07-18 (external critique)

Five verified defects fixed. Each was checked against a source, not assumed.

| Defect | Was | Now |
|---|---|---|
| Deck-count column | numbers from an orphan file with no builder; 15 of 76 rows exceeded the castable ceiling | recomputed from color identity vs the 25 archetypes, all 76 rows |
| Default sort | rank 2 was a card in **0** decks, on a page titled "by Deck Count" | sorted by the corrected count, descending, ranks renumbered |
| 2026 rotation | claimed WOE and LCI rotate fall 2026 | **no Standard rotation in 2026**; moves to first release of 2027 |
| Vault reward | "3 rare + 1 mythic" | **3 uncommon, 2 rare, 1 mythic** |
| Collection % | 59.7% beside 10,960 / 17,956 | **61.0%**; the old figure was 10,735/17,956, never updated after the count was corrected |

### Why the deck counts were withdrawn

The column drew from `~/Projects/_outputs/mtga-mardu/most_shared_real.json`, which has no
builder script anywhere on disk and no recorded denominator. Its counts are impossible
against a 25-archetype field: Sacred Foundry (W/R) read **24 / 25** when only **6** of the
tracked archetypes can cast a W/R card at all. Same-color cards also disagreed with each
other (W/R cards read 11, 21, and 24), which cannot happen under either stated metric.
Those numbers were almost certainly measured against a much larger all-Standard deck pool
and then printed against a denominator of 25.

The replacement is exactly computable from `data.json` and is what the method section
already documented: how many of the 25 archetypes **can** play the card. The column is now
labelled "Decks that can play it" rather than "Most shared crafts", because it counts
eligibility, not observed play. A real observed-play number needs a source with a known
denominator; it is queued, not faked.

Corrected extremes: colorless cards sit at 25 / 25, B/R lands at 9, W/B lands at 8,
W/R lands at 6.

